[
  "[SCENE: Camera on desk setup. MacBook Air visible with Dead Rabbit Radio sticker.]\nThis is Studio Workbench. I'm Mike Hall.\nLast time, we looked at the eighteen-year Ship of Theseus: how a wooden boat of shell scripts became today's media pipeline.\nToday, we're looking at the single most important plank in that ship.\nIt starts with this sticker right here on the lid of my MacBook Air, next to the Apple logo.\n[PAUSE]\n[SHOW: Close-up of Dead Rabbit Radio sticker on MacBook lid.]\nIt's from Dead Rabbit Radio, a daily paranormal podcast hosted by Jason Carpenter.\nBefore we look at the code, let me show you something I found in the transcript archive.\n[SHOW: ep_0769_travis_scott_comp.srt at 00:00:49 and 00:14:53.]\nNovember 8, 2021. Seventeen months before I wrote a single line of Fluffle code.\nJason welcomed me into Dead Rabbit Command as a new Patreon supporter.\nHe had me flying the Carpenter Helicopter, which Whisper small famously heard as \"carbon helicopter.\"\nIn show lore, Jason calls his supporters The Fluffle, because a group of rabbits is called a fluffle.\n[FREEFORM: Mention why that name stuck when spinning up the five Pis in April 2023.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Show just3ws/many-hands-make-light-work (2014) beside just3ws/fluffle (2023).]\nPeople ask why I didn't test this first on the UGtastic interviews.\nI had over two hundred Chicago community conversations sitting on hard drives.\nI'd already failed once in 2014 with a crowdsourcing repo called many-hands-make-light-work.\nCloud speech APIs cost thousands of dollars back then, and those interviews are irreplaceable history.\nI wasn't going to beta-test an unproven local AI pipeline on UGtastic.\n[FREEFORM: Explain what Dead Rabbit Radio gave you: one consistent speaker, over a thousand episodes, and zero risk to the archive.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Pop out the Interactive Architecture Canvas on /series/studio-workbench/fluffle-cluster/.]\nLet's walk through what I actually built in April and May 2023.\nThe control plane was my 15-inch 2018 Intel Core i7 MacBook Pro.\nCurb and Nokogiri pulled the Libsyn RSS feed and parsed each episode GUID.\nDown followed HTTP redirects to grab the MP3, and FFmpeg turned it into 16-kilohertz mono WAV.\n[SHOW: Rakefile lines 185-221 and bin/transcribe.]\nFrom there, Rakefile used Net::SSH to check on five Raspberry Pi 4s on my desk: node01 through node05.\nTwo 4Bs and three plain 4s, running 64-bit DietPi on Debian 11 off USB thumbdrives.\nBefore shipping a single file, it checked two hard limits: five gigabytes free, and under two gigabytes of audio per node.\nOn each Pi, whisper.cpp ran the small model, deleted the WAV to protect the thumbdrive, and slept two minutes so the passive heatsinks could cool down.\n[PAUSE]\nNotice there was no Redis here. Just dotfile markers right on the filesystem.\nDot-downloading, dot-downloaded, dot-converted, dot-distributed, dot-failed.\n[FREEFORM: Connect those 2023 dotfiles directly to today's five-state Maildir queue in media-pipeline.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Show fluffle/README.md and fluffle/replacements.yaml.]\nRemember in Episode 1 when we talked about Peter Watts' Blindsight and Cunningham's bees?\nNeural models extrude tokens without knowing what the world actually means.\nWhisper had zero idea who Jason Carpenter was.\nIn README.md and replacements.yaml, I logged over thirty different ways it spelled his name.\nGarpenter. Garbineiro. Krupper. Carmoner. Garbender. Even Jason Asa.\nIt even transcribed \"Dead Rabbit Dirigible\" as \"deadrabberdurgible.\"\n[SHOW: Git timestamps for May 17, 2023 (07:03 to 07:13 AM).]\nAt 7:03 AM on May 17, I wrote bin/replacements.\nTen minutes later, one deterministic dictionary pass cleaned 981 files at once.\n[FREEFORM: Reflect on why probabilistic AI still needs deterministic ground truth downstream.]\n",
  "[SCENE: Show git log of fluffle (79 commits) and bin/episode_checker.rb.]\nIf you read all seventy-nine commits across sixty-seven days, you see the real pace of the project.\nPhase one was pure joy: fifty-one commits in twelve days.\nRunning batches at six in the morning before work, at lunch, and at nine at night.\nOn April 8, I caught a whisper.cpp naming quirk and deleted twenty-six duplicate SRT files.\nThen on April 25, I tried hand-editing five hundred and nine transcripts.\nI realized manual cleanup wouldn't scale, and I walked away for twenty-two days.\n[PAUSE]\nWhen I came back in May, I built the Rakefile state machine and episode_checker.rb so I could see all 1,175 rows in one green-and-red HTML table.\n[FREEFORM: Mention the April 6 DM draft to Jason Carpenter, chatting on Discord, and keeping it private off-air.]\nOn June 12, commit seventy-nine tried to generalize across other podcasts like Belief Hole and Philosophize This.\nBroken XML feeds, day-job incidents, and solo maintenance hit at once, and I stopped.\n",
  "[SCENE: Side-by-side of fluffle/episodes.html and 2026 media-pipeline cockpit on M4.]\nOn git log alone, Fluffle looks like an abandoned experiment.\nIn reality, every survival rule in our 2026 pipeline was earned on those five hot Raspberry Pis.\nThe dotfiles became our five-state Maildir queue.\nThe WAV scratch files became ephemeral memory streaming.\nAnd those five Pis gave way to Apple Silicon M4 transcribing twenty-eight minutes of speech in 103 seconds.\n[PAUSE]\nThank you to Jason Carpenter for making Dead Rabbit Radio, and for being generous when I reached out.\nIf you want to explore the repo, the canvas, or the architecture notes, everything is linked on just3ws.com.\nNext, we carry these transcripts straight into The Sound Above rewatch.\nUntil then, keep your feedback loops fast, and take care of each other.\n"
]
